<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Frum movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1930s–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The John Frum movement is a religious movement on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanna">Tanna</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a>, often described as a cargo cult. It arose in the late 1930s around a figure called John Frum, who promised the islanders wealth if they abandoned missionary rule and returned to custom. The arrival of large American forces in the New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> strengthened the belief, and followers still hold an annual celebration on 15 February.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-frum-movement.jpg" alt="John Frum movement" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flickr user Charmaine Tham · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Frum_gathering_area.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
